'''Yoldia''' (yōl′di-ä), ''n.'' [NL. (Möller, 1842), named after Count ''Yoldi'' of Sweden.] A genus of bivalves, of the family ''Nuculidæ'' (or ''Ledidæ''), related to the ark-shells. {{smaller|The several species are of boreal distribution; they resemble the members of the genus ''Leda,'' but have long slender siphons, a compressed long oval shell, beaked and slightly gaping behind, and covered with shining epidermis. ''Y. arctica'', ''Y. limatula'', and ''Y. thraciæformis'' are examples; the latter is found in deep water off the New England coast.}}

'''yolding''' (yōl′ding), ''n.'' Same as ''yoldring.''—'''Yellow yolding.''' {{smaller|Same as ''yellowhammer'', 1.}}

'''yoldring, yoldrin''' (yōl′dring, -drin), ''n.'' Same as ''yeldring'', ''yowley''. [Prov. Eng.]
{{smaller block|But you heed me no more than a goss-hawk minds a yellow ''yoldring.''
{{right|''Scott'', Abbot, xvii.}}}}

